WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.949
Safe request construction preserves structure.

2
00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:08.965
URLs, schemes, hosts, ports, paths, query parameters, headers, content types, and request bodies all have syntax and meaning.

3
00:00:08.965 --> 00:00:16.371
When code builds those pieces with casual string concatenation, unexpected characters, encodings, separators, or whitespace can create requests that

4
00:00:16.371 --> 00:00:18.710
differ from what the developer intended.

5
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:24.167
Prefer library-supported URL, query, header, and body construction APIs where they are available.

6
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:28.454
These APIs help keep data in the field where it belongs.

7
00:00:28.454 --> 00:00:32.352
A query value should be encoded as a query value.

8
00:00:32.352 --> 00:00:36.250
A header value should be validated as a header value.

9
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:42.877
A JSON body should be serialized by a structured serializer, not assembled by hand from untrusted fragments.

10
00:00:42.877 --> 00:00:44.826
Destination handling deserves special care.

11
00:00:44.826 --> 00:00:52.621
If callers can influence the URL, host, port, path, or scheme, the application should decide whether that flexibility is allowed.

12
00:00:52.621 --> 00:00:56.909
Some clients should contact only a fixed service from trusted configuration.

13
00:00:56.909 --> 00:01:00.027
Others may support a bounded set of destinations.

14
00:01:00.027 --> 00:01:07.433
Values from files, environment variables, plugins, command-line options, queues, or other services should not become trusted destinations without

15
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:07.823
validation.

16
00:01:07.823 --> 00:01:10.551
Keep trusted configuration separate from external input.

17
00:01:10.551 --> 00:01:17.568
A base service endpoint, credential scope, or tenant boundary should not be quietly replaced by user-controlled text.

18
00:01:17.568 --> 00:01:24.974
When request fields combine trusted and untrusted values, document the rules and validate the final structured form before sending.

19
00:01:24.974 --> 00:01:30.431
Request construction is easier to review when the code says what each value means.

20
00:01:30.431 --> 00:01:39.006
The safest clients make schemes, hosts, headers, body formats, encodings, and content types explicit rather than hiding protocol text inside a string.

21
00:01:39.006 --> 00:01:47.191
This also improves diagnostics: when a request is rejected, the team can tell whether the problem was destination policy, encoding, authentication,

22
00:01:47.191 --> 00:01:49.920
content type, or an application-level rule.
